+    {
+      "key": "signed-overlap-markov-obstruction",
+      "namespace": "probability.open-problems",
+      "logical_name": "w339-signed-overlap-markov-obstruction",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Vanishing signed mean overlap does not justify Markov or a second-moment bound",
+      "statement": "For X=(Z,-Z), Z standard normal, and its Ornstein-Uhlenbeck copy Y=(Z_t,-Z_t), where Z_t=e^(-t)Z+sqrt(1-e^(-2t))Z prime, the covariance overlap R(I(X),I(Y)) equals 1 when the signs agree and -1 otherwise. Its mean is (2/pi)arcsin(e^(-t)), tending to zero as t tends to infinity, while its second moment is exactly one and P(R>1/2) tends to 1/2. Thus a small mean of a signed overlap alone cannot be used with Markov to conclude a small upper tail. This does not refute chaos-to-multiple-peaks at vanishing noise time for a sequence of fields.",
+      "proof_text": "The covariance matrix is [[1,-1],[-1,1]], positive semidefinite, so this is a valid Gaussian field. Its maximum index is determined by the sign of Z (ties have probability zero), making the overlap the product of signs. Represent the correlated normal pair by two projections of an isotropic two-dimensional normal vector: its uniform angle gives sign-disagreement probability arccos(e^(-t))/pi. Therefore E R=1-2 arccos(e^(-t))/pi=(2/pi)arcsin(e^(-t)), and R^2=1. The limiting positive-tail probability is 1/2. The noise times here tend to infinity; the conclusion only invalidates the signed-Markov step in C0122-A001, not the original short-noise conjecture.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "signed covariance overlap",
+        "this diagnostic uses t tending to infinity"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-campaign-w339-20261010:signed-overlap-markov-obstruction",
+        "url": "~/brain_workers/w339/CLAIMS.md",
+        "revision": "unreviewed-2026-10-10-v1",
