+    {
+      "key": "sir-scaled-lifetimes-fail-inclusion",
+      "namespace": "probability.open-problems",
+      "logical_name": "w339-sir-scaled-lifetimes-fail-inclusion",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Scaled exponential infectious lifetimes can reduce the infected set on a fixed contact schedule",
+      "statement": "In a four-person SIR model with irreversible removal and certain transmission on a listed contact, let A be initially infectious and allow contacts only A-C at time 0.1, A-B at 1, C-B at 9, and B-D at 10. Coupling iid rate-one exponential infectious lifetimes L_A,L_C,L_B to the longer lifetimes 3L_A,3L_C,3L_B gives positive probability that D is infected in the baseline process but never infected in the longer-lifetime process. Therefore this shared-lifetime coupling does not have pathwise inclusion of infected sets. This is a fixed-contact diagnostic, not a counterexample to Brownian SIR survival or survival monotonicity.",
+      "proof_text": "Take the open lifetime box .45<L_A<.55, 11.5<L_C<12.5, and 1.8<L_B<2.2. In the baseline A infects C at .1, is removed before 1, and C survives to infect B at 9. B is still infectious at 10 and infects D. Under scaling by three, A survives to infect B at 1; B is removed between 6.4 and 7.6, cannot be reinfected at 9, and cannot infect D at 10. C survives to 9 in both cases. The open box has positive probability, equal to the product of the three differences of exponential tails. This extends the prior deterministic lifetime example to an explicit positive-probability exponential-removal coupling. The Brownian contact realization is not established.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "fixed contact schedule",
+        "irreversible removal",
+        "certain transmission at contacts",
+        "lifetimes measured from infection"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
